import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { TableHero } from "../../demos/table/hero.rx"
import { TableColumns } from "../../demos/table/columns.rx"
import { TableGrouped } from "../../demos/table/grouped.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Table" subtitle="A data table on a native table element: sorting, filtering, selection, paging, column sizing and grouping, with TanStack Table keeping the state.">
    <Demo source="table/hero"><TableHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      <code>createTable</code> makes the table: an object that holds the state and works out the rows. You loop
      over its header groups, rows and cells and write the elements; each part takes the object it is about
      as a prop.
    </p>
    <Code file="table/anatomy.rx.txt" />
    <p>
      The table needs one package that the rest of Bisque doesn't. Install it, then copy Bisque again:{" "}
      <code>bisque copy</code> leaves Table out of an app that doesn't have the package.
    </p>
    <Code file="table/install.sh.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The table itself. <code>{"<table>"}</code>, <code>{"<thead>"}</code>, <code>{"<tr>"}</code>,{" "}
      <code>{"<th>"}</code> and <code>{"<td>"}</code> give assistive technology the rows, the columns and which
      header belongs to which cell, so there is no Root part and no context, and the row and cell parts add
      only <code>data-*</code> attributes to style by. The controls are native too: a sort trigger and an expand
      trigger are buttons, selection and column visibility are checkboxes, the filters are inputs.
    </p>
    <p>
      What a data table needs beyond that is state and the rows computed from it. That is{" "}
      <a href="https://tanstack.com/table">TanStack Table</a>'s core, version 9, which is headless and
      framework-agnostic. Version 9 lets its host supply the reactivity, and here every piece of table state
      is a Solid signal: an equation or a piece of markup that reads <code>column.getIsSorted()</code> depends
      on the sorting and on nothing else. Everything the core can do is available on the table object; its{" "}
      <a href="https://tanstack.com/table/latest/docs">documentation</a> is the reference for the options and
      methods.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no grid navigation: the table is a <code>table</code>, not a <code>grid</code>, and each control
      in it is an ordinary <kbd>Tab</kbd> stop.
    </p>

    <h2 id="differences">What differs from TanStack Table elsewhere</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Column definitions hold values, not markup.</strong> ReactiveScript has no markup outside a view,
        so there is no <code>flexRender</code>. Render <code>column.columnDef.header</code> and{" "}
        <code>cell.getValue()</code>, and branch on <code>cell.column.id</code> for a cell with markup of its
        own, as the demo above does for the status and the checkbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An <code>on…Change</code> option receives the new value</strong>, never an updater function, in a
        change of its own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Loop over row ids.</strong> A <code>for</code> keeps a row's element while the thing it loops
        over stays the same, and the core makes new row objects whenever the data changes. An id is the same
        string before and after, so the row's element, and focus inside it, survive.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never keep a table, row, column or cell in a <code>source</code> or a keyed equation.</strong>{" "}
        Both wrap the object in a store, and the core's methods fail on the copy. Data and state can be held in
        a <code>source</code>: <code>createTable</code> hands the core a plain copy when one changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rows are not typed by your data.</strong> ReactiveScript has no generics, so{" "}
        <code>row.original</code> and <code>cell.getValue()</code> are <code>any</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="sorting">Sorting</h3>
    <p>
      <code>TableSortTrigger</code> is a button for the column's header. A press sorts ascending, then
      descending, then not at all; a column of numbers starts descending. With <kbd>Shift</kbd> held it adds the
      column to the sort. The header cell carries <code>aria-sort</code> while its column is sorted, and both
      carry <code>data-sorted</code> to draw the arrow from. Set <code>enableSorting: false</code> on a column
      definition and its trigger is disabled.
    </p>

    <h3 id="filtering">Filtering</h3>
    <p>
      <code>TableGlobalFilter</code> is a search input over every column; <code>TableColumnFilter</code> filters
      by one. Both write the table's state on every keystroke, and a filter returns the table to its first page.
      Leave a column out of the search with <code>enableGlobalFilter: false</code>. For a filter that isn't a
      text input (a select of statuses, a range), call <code>column.setFilterValue(value)</code> from a control
      of your own and give the column a <code>filterFn</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="selection">Selecting rows</h3>
    <p>
      <code>TableSelectRow</code> and <code>TableSelectAll</code> are Bisque checkboxes wired to the table.
      Select-all is mixed while only some rows are selected, and stands for every row or, with{" "}
      <code>scope="page"</code>, for the rows on the current page. <kbd>Shift</kbd> with a press selects the
      range from the last row pressed. The selection is{" "}
      <code>table.getSelectedRowModel().rows</code>; it is kept by row id, so pass <code>getRowId</code> or it
      follows row positions.
    </p>

    <h3 id="pagination">Pagination</h3>
    <p>
      <code>TablePagination</code> is a <a href="/components/pagination/">Pagination</a> root whose page is the
      table's: put Pagination's own parts inside it. The page size is{" "}
      <code>table.setPageSize(n)</code>, from a native select in the demo.
    </p>

    <h3 id="columns">Column sizes, pinning and visibility</h3>
    <p>
      Each header and body cell carries its column's size as <code>--column-size</code>, and a pinned column's
      offset as <code>--column-start</code> or <code>--column-end</code>. The parts set no layout: a stylesheet
      that wants sized columns uses <code>table-layout: fixed</code> and <code>width: var(--column-size)</code>,
      and one that wants pinned columns makes <code>[data-pinned]</code> cells sticky at the offset.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>TableColumnResizer</code> is a handle to drag. It is also a focusable separator: the arrow keys
      resize by 10 pixels, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> go to the column's smallest and largest size, and
      a double click puts the size back. <code>TableColumnToggle</code>, in the first demo, is a checkbox that
      shows and hides a column.
    </p>
    <Demo source="table/columns"><TableColumns /></Demo>
    <div class="note">
      Pinning state needs both sides: <code>{"columnPinning: { start: [\"name\"], end: [] }"}</code>. The core
      reads <code>end</code> without checking that it is there.
    </div>

    <h3 id="grouping">Grouping, aggregation and expanding</h3>
    <p>
      Group by one or more columns and the table's rows become group rows with their rows under them.{" "}
      <code>TableExpandTrigger</code> shows and hides what is under a row, and says so with{" "}
      <code>aria-expanded</code>; it does the same for a tree (<code>getSubRows</code>) and for a detail row you
      render while <code>row.getIsExpanded()</code>. A column with an <code>aggregationFn</code> shows the
      group's aggregate in the group's row.
    </p>
    <Demo source="table/grouped"><TableGrouped /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling state, and data from a server</h3>
    <p>
      Every piece of state can be held by the app: pass it in <code>state</code> with its{" "}
      <code>on…Change</code>. The table then shows what it is given, so an owner that doesn't write the new value
      keeps the table where it was. With an <code>on…Change</code> and no <code>state</code>, the table keeps the
      state and the handler only listens.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is how a server does the sorting and paging: the request is a question of the state, and the{" "}
      <code>manual…</code> options turn the table's own stages off.
    </p>
    <Code file="table/controlled.rx.txt" />
    <p>
      While a page is loading, the table's own controls show the new state at once (the sorted header, the
      current page) and the rows stay until the answer arrives. The table reads its options ahead of a change
      that is still in flight, so an app action that is saving never freezes it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="features">A smaller bundle</h3>
    <p>
      <code>createTable</code> registers every feature the core has, so that any option works without setup,
      and the bundle carries all of them. The core is tree-shakeable by feature: build a <code>features</code>{" "}
      set in a JavaScript module, pass it as the <code>features</code> option (it is read once), and only those
      features are bundled. The row, cell and header parts leave out what a missing feature would say; a
      control needs its feature registered, and so does a method you call yourself:{" "}
      <code>row.getVisibleCells()</code> is the column visibility feature's, and without it the cells are{" "}
      <code>row.getAllCells()</code>.
    </p>
    <Code file="table/features.js.txt" />

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="table" />
  </DocsPage>
}
